2026-07-29T07:39:48.9940983Z [36;1m# Compile on every core the runner has, not the two this used to pin.[0m
2026-07-29T07:39:48.9941437Z [36;1m# Standard GitHub-hosted Linux runners have been 4-vCPU since 2024, so[0m
2026-07-29T07:39:48.9941891Z [36;1m# -j2 left half the box idle through the phase that dominates a build:[0m
2026-07-29T07:39:48.9942323Z [36;1m# measured on a 4-core box, 8 vendored unity objects with ccache[0m
2026-07-29T07:39:48.9942739Z [36;1m# disabled take 70.8 s at -j2 and 43.1 s at -j4 -- 1.64x, at identical[0m
2026-07-29T07:39:48.9943182Z [36;1m# user time. Peak RSS per unity object is ~834 MB, so four in flight is[0m
2026-07-29T07:39:48.9943538Z [36;1m# ~3.3 GB of the runner's 16 GB.[0m
2026-07-29T07:39:48.9943882Z [36;1m# detectCores() can return NA on exotic platforms; fall back to the old[0m
2026-07-29T07:39:48.9944334Z [36;1m# value there. `--as-cran` sets _R_CHECK_LIMIT_CORES_, but that polices[0m
2026-07-29T07:39:48.9944801Z [36;1m# the *package's* runtime parallelism, not compilation, so this is safe.[0m
2026-07-29T07:39:48.9945388Z [36;1mncores="$(Rscript --vanilla -e 'n <- parallel::detectCores(); cat(if (is.na(n) || n < 1) 2L else as.integer(n))')"[0m
2026-07-29T07:39:48.9945903Z [36;1mecho "MAKEFLAGS = -j${ncores}" >> ~/.R/Makevars[0m
